This man ate an entire airplane. Not a toy. A real airplane. Piece by piece.

His name was Michel Lotito, an entertainer from Grenoble, France. People called him Monsieur Mangetout. Mister Eats Everything.

But wait. How is that even possible?

It starts with a rare condition called pica: an urge to eat things that are not food. Metal. Glass. Rubber.

Michel turned it into a stage show. Over the years, he ate eighteen bicycles, fifteen shopping carts, seven TVs, six chandeliers, and even a coffin. Handles included.

Then, in 1978, he started on a Cessna 150. A real plane, with wings, wheels and an engine.

Picture the whole plane cut into tiny pieces, small enough to swallow. Every day, about two pounds of metal. He gulped them down like pills, with mineral oil and lots of water.

It took him about two years.

So why didn't it kill him? Doctors x-rayed his stomach and found its lining was unusually thick. Like a pipe with armor plating inside.

And here's the weird part: bananas and hard-boiled eggs made him sick.

Guinness World Records gave him a brass plaque for the strangest diet. He ate that too.

Doctors are clear: never try this. Swallowing metal can tear your insides apart.

So yes, a man really ate an airplane. Two years, one tiny piece at a time.

